This series of posts follows a new video by "La Fisica che non ti aspetti" about tides and tidal forces. As the contents have become much larger than initially I may have thought approaching this problem, they are split in different articles. First, the dynamics of the Earth is described in the Earth-Moon system, whose center of mass is assumed to be the origin of an inertial reference frame. An observer on the Earth is thus non-inertial. The combination of fictitious forces and gravitational attraction from the Moon is found to be the cause of the tides, and this term is represented here under the - quite rough - assumption of stationary sea, solving a problem of fluid statics.
Then dynamics is introduced with increasing levels of complexity, using the equations of shallow waters: first the frequency response of a straight channel under the action of an external forcing is studied; then the dynamics of the sea covering the whole surface of an *aquaplanet* Earth in the Earth-Moon system is discussed; eventually, the evolution of an approximate model of real basin is discussed.

