This post follows a new video by "La Fisica che non ti aspetti" about tides and tidal forces.

**Attraction from the Moon to the points of the Earth - inertial reference frame.**

A point $P$ with mass $m$ is subject to the gravitational force due to the Moon and the Earth $\mathbf{F} = m \mathbf{g}$, with the force per unit mass $\mathbf{g}$ given by

$$\mathbf{g}^{(0)}(P) = G M_E \frac{C_E - P}{|C_E - P|^3} + G M_M \frac{C_M - P}{|C_M - P|^3}$$

**Non-inertial Earth reference frame.** Using the laws of [relative kinematics](https://basics2022.github.io/bbooks-physics-mechanics/ch/kinematics-relative.html), from the point of view of a non-inertial reference frame rotating with the Earth, the description of the problem involves *apparent forces* due to the motion of the non-inertial reference frame. The total force perceived in this reference frame is

$$\mathbf{g}^{(1)} = \mathbf{g}^{(0)} - \mathbf{a}^{(0)}_{Q/O} - \boldsymbol\alpha_{1/0} \times \mathbf{r}_{QP} - 2 \boldsymbol\omega_{1/0} \times \mathbf{v}^{(1)}_{P/Q} - \boldsymbol\omega_{1/0} \times \boldsymbol\omega_{1/0} \times \mathbf{r}_{QP} \ .$$

## Simplified model

Here the motion of the Earth around the Sun is neglected, and only the rotation around its axis is taken into account. Thus, the acceleration of the center of the Earth w.r.t. an inertial reference frame is approximately zero, $\mathbf{a}_{C_E O} \sim \mathbf{0}$.
Now, let's furhter assume:

* the angular velocity of the Earth is constant, $\boldsymbol\alpha_{1/0} = \mathbf{0}$
* the axis of rotation of the Earth is orthogonal to the plane of the orbit of the Moon around the Earth, $\boldsymbol\Omega_{1/0} = \Omega \hat{\mathbf{z}}$
* a point $P$ on Earth surface approximately moves with the surface itself.  The relative velocity is thus $\mathbf{v}^{(1)}_{P/Q} \sim \mathbf{0}$, while its position w.r.t. the center of the Earth $C_E$ is $\mathbf{r}_{E P} = R_E \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P$
* the relative position of the center of the Moon w.r.t. the center of the Earth is defined as $\mathbf{R}_{EM} = R_{EM} \hat{\mathbf{R}}$

Thus, the acceleration perceived in the non-inertial reference frame is

$$\begin{aligned}
  \mathbf{g}^{(1)}
  & \sim \mathbf{g}^{(0)} - \boldsymbol\Omega_{1/0} \times \boldsymbol\Omega_{1/0} \times \mathbf{r}_{E P} = \\
  & = \mathbf{g}^{(0)}_E + \mathbf{g}^{(0)}_M - \boldsymbol\Omega_{1/0} \times \boldsymbol\Omega_{1/0} \times \mathbf{r}_{E P} \ ,
\end{aligned}$$

distinguishing between the radial contribution $\mathbf{g}^{(0)}$ due to the gravitational attraction from the Earth from the other non-uniform contributions. The non-uniform contributions read

* Centrifugal force

  $$\begin{aligned}
    - \boldsymbol\Omega \times \boldsymbol\Omega \times \mathbf{r}_{EP} 
    & = - \Omega^2 R_{E} \hat{\mathbf{z}} \times \hat{\mathbf{z}} \times \mathbf{r}_{P} = \\
    & = - \Omega^2 R_{E} \sin \phi \, \hat{\mathbf{z}} \times \boldsymbol\theta_P  = \\
    & =   \Omega^2 R_{E} \sin \phi \, \boldsymbol\rho_P \ .
  \end{aligned}$$

* Gravitational attraction from the Moon

   $$\begin{aligned}
     \mathbf{g}^{(0)}_{M} 
       = G M_M \frac{P - C_M}{| P - C_M |^3} 
       = \frac{G M_M}{R_{EM}^2} \frac{ \hat{\mathbf{R}} + \frac{R_E}{R_{EM}} \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P }{\left| \hat{\mathbf{R}} + \frac{R_E}{R_{EM}} \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P \right|^3}
       \sim \frac{G M_M}{R_{EM}^2} \left\{ \hat{\mathbf{R}} - 3 \frac{R_E}{R_{EM}} \hat{\mathbf{R}} \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P \right\}
   \end{aligned}$$




## Some algebra

### Power series expansion of the gravitational force from the Moon

Expanding for small $a := \tfrac{R_{E}}{R_{EM}}$,

$$\begin{aligned}
  \left.\mathbf{f}(a)\right|_{a=0} & = \hat{\mathbf{R}} \\
  \left.\mathbf{f}'(a)\right|_{a=0} & = \left. \frac{\hat{\mathbf{r}}_P | \dots |^3 - 3 | \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P | \left( \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P \right) \left( \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P \right) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P }{| \dots |^6} \right|_{a=0} = \\
  & = \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P - 3 \hat{\mathbf{R}} \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{r}}_P
\end{aligned}$$

since

$$\begin{aligned}
  \frac{d}{d a} | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} |^3 & = 3 | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} |^2 \frac{d}{da} | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} |  \\
  & = 3 \hat{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \left( \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}} \right) | \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}} |
\end{aligned}$$

as

$$\begin{aligned}
  \frac{d}{d a} | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} |^2 & = 2 | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} | \frac{d}{da} | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} | 
    = 2 \hat{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \left( \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}} \right) \\
  \frac{d}{d a} | \hat{\mathbf{R}} \cdot a \hat{\mathbf{r}} | & = \hat{\mathbf{r}} \cdot \frac{\hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}}}{\left| \hat{\mathbf{R}} + a \hat{\mathbf{r}} \right|}
\end{aligned}$$

